<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=mobile-phone">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Communication and computing</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mobile phone</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1973">1973</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as cell phone, mobile telephone, cellular telephone</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A mobile phone is a portable <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telephone">telephone</a> that makes and receives calls over a radio link while its user moves about; the first handheld mobile phone was demonstrated by Martin Cooper of Motorola in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> City on 3 April 1973, with a handset weighing about 2 kilograms. Earlier mobile phones had to be installed in vehicles. Nippon <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">Telegraph</a> and Telephone launched the first cellular network in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in 1979, the DynaTAC 8000x of 1983 was the first handheld phone on sale, and by 2024 subscriptions worldwide exceeded 9.1 billion.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mobile-phone.jpg" alt="Mobile phone" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rico Shen · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2007Computex_e21Forum-MartinCooper.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
